# ===[ Why Fondation models for tabular data are difficult ]===================
- Language has 'Words'
- Images have 'pixels'
- Tabular data has ... 'Everything'
  . Exmaple:
      """ Hospital
        Age
        Height
        Weight
        Diagnosis
      """
      """ Bank 
        Income 
        Balance 
        Credit Score 
      """
      ...
  - There is no 'universal vocalbulary'
  - 'Every dataset' has 'different meanings' 
  ->> [ That's Why tabular foundation models are much harder than language models ]

  |> [ Why Tree Models have dominated? ] 
     - For nearly '20 years': if someone says
       "I have tabular data"
     - people immediately think
       . XGBoost
       . LightGBM
       . CAtBoost 
     - Because tree ensembles are incredibly good at
       . Mixed features types
       . missing values
       . nonlinearities 
       . small datasets
       . noisy features
     -> 'Neural networks usually lose on tabular datasets'
     -> 'That is why computer vision became dominated by transformers' 
     -> 'Language become domincated by Transformer'
     -> 'Tabular data stayed dominated by gradient boosting'

# ===[ What is TabICLv2? ]=====================================================
- Think of it as:
  """
    A pretrained Transformer that has already learned how to solve thousands
    (or millions) of tabular learning problems before you even use it.
  """
- Instead of saying:
  """
    Learn from my dataset
  """
- You say: 
  """
    Here are examples
    Predict this new row
  """
->> That is completely different philosophy
  
- Traditional ML:
      """
        Dataset
          |
        Training
          |
        Model
      """

- TabICLv2
      """
        pretrained Foundation model
        |
        Dataset as Context
        |
        Predictions
      """
->> 'The knowledge already exists inside the model'

  |> [ Why is it called a foundation Model? ]
     - A foundation model is trained 'once' on an 'enormous and diverse set '
       'of taks so it can later adapt to many new tasks with little or no'
       'additional training'.
     - Example:
       . 'GPT' -> language
       . 'CLIP' -> vision-language
       . 'SAM' -> image segmentation
       . 'TabICLv2' -> tabular data

  # INFO:[ VERY VERY IMPORTANT ]===============================================
  # - The important point is that the training happens before you eve download
  #   the model, when you use it, you are leveraging the knowledge learned
  #   during that massive pretraining phase.
  # ===========================================================================


  |> [ Who Created TabICLv2? ] 
     - 'TabCLIv2' was introduced by researchers from the 'inria' and
       'collaborating institutions', it extends the original 'TabICL' work
       with improvements in scalability, training methodology, and performance,
       aiming to create a true foundation model for tabular machine learning

     - The motivation was simple:
        """
          Can we build a single pretrained model that performs well across
          thousands of tabular datasets without requiring dataset-specific
           hyperparameter turning?
        """
        -> Instead of spending hours tunning 'XGBoost' or 'CatBoost' for every
           new dataset, the researchers wanted a model that already 'understands'
           tabular prediction problems


